# Test: a link flaps while a config commit holds the lock the link worker
#       needs, and the running daemon still ends with its default route at the
#       metric live carrier calls for -- without the self-heal repairing it.
#
# VALIDATES: spec-fixit-link-event-drop-loses-carrier-state AC-5, on a RUNNING
#            daemon. Every part of the path is production wiring: the netlink
#            monitor's read loop, the subscribers subscribeLinkEvents registers
#            (internal/component/iface/link_queue.go), the worker runEngine
#            starts on the same queue, and the route calls applyLinkEvent makes.
# PREVENTS:  the unrecoverable loss. Those subscribers used to push into a
#            16-deep channel with a `default:` branch that dropped on a full
#            buffer, and a config commit was when it filled: OnConfigApply holds
#            dhcpMu across reconcileDHCP while it stops and starts DHCP clients,
#            and the worker takes that same lock for every apply. The route
#            handlers are idempotent by routeMetricState, so nothing in the
#            event stream repairs a state that was dropped.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING THE ROUTE ASSERTION. The route metric ALONE cannot
# discriminate this defect on a running daemon, and a test that asserted only
# the metric would be vacuous. Phase 3 of the same spec added the carrier
# resync: the rate tracker pushes live carrier state every second
# (SubscribeCollectNotify, internal/component/iface/register.go) and
# resyncCarrierState (link_queue.go) repairs any interface whose acted-on route
# metric contradicts it, so the END STATE converges within about a second
# whether or not one event survived. What separates the two producers is
# whether that self-heal had to fire. Each round therefore asserts three
# things, and the test needs all three:
#
#   1. the kernel's ::/0 route on zeflapv0 reaches 254 + 1024
#      AC-5's own words: the metric matches live carrier after the commit.
#   2. ze_iface_link_events_coalesced_total{name=zeflapv0} rose this round
#      The burst really did outrun the worker. Without this the test goes
#      quietly vacuous the day the drain keeps up, and a queue under no
#      pressure says nothing about a queue that drops. Measured 85-100 per
#      round on the arm64 QEMU VM.
#   3. ze_iface_carrier_resyncs_total{name=zeflapv0} never moves
#      No transition was lost: the event path alone carried the link to its
#      final state. This is the assertion the dropping producer fails.
#
# (3) is an absence, and an absence is evidence only when the mechanism behind
# it is alive. It is: TestCarrierResyncRepairsAContradictedRouteMetric
# (internal/component/iface/route_metric_test.go) drives resyncCarrierState into
# a repair and reads countCarrierResync, and (2) proves the same metric family
# is registered and scraped in THIS run, so a zero here is a measured zero and
# not a missing registry.
#
# MEASURED, 2026-08-22, arm64 QEMU VM, against a queue restored to the pre-fix
# producer (a 16-deep FIFO of individual events, drop on full, no coalescing,
# with the drop still counted so the run fails on (3) rather than on a missing
# counter):
#
#   producer      resync delta, rounds 0..5
#   fixed queue   0 0 0 0 0 0
#   pre-fix FIFO  0 1 1 0 1 1
#
# The pre-fix producer loses the final transition in about two rounds in three,
# not in every one: when the burst ends the buffer usually has room again, so a
# single burst is not enough. Six rounds put the chance of missing the
# regression at (1/3)^6, which is 0.14%. That is why this test runs rounds
# rather than one burst, and why the round count must not be cut.
#
# WHY THE COMMIT IS A REAL CONFIG CHANGE. Each round flips a DHCP hostname in
# the config file before the SIGHUP, so the reload reaches reconcileDHCP and
# stops and starts all forty clients under dhcpMu. A SIGHUP over an UNCHANGED
# config does not reach the iface component's apply at all and the worker is
# never blocked: measured, that variant recorded zero applies. The client count
# is what makes the hold long enough to cover a burst -- measured 45-86ms with
# one client, 224-335ms with four, 1.1-3.3s with forty.
#
# WHY 101 TRANSITIONS AND NOT MORE. The upper bound is the netlink socket, not
# the queue. LinkSubscribe (internal/plugins/iface/netlink/monitor_linux.go)
# sends into a 64-deep Go channel from a goroutine that blocks on it, so a
# burst the monitor cannot read fast enough overflows the KERNEL's socket
# buffer and the transition is lost before ze sees it. That loss is outside
# this spec and it makes (3) unattainable for any producer: at 401 transitions
# it was measured. The driver reads /proc/net/netlink and fails the run if it
# happens.
#
# The stimulus for the default route is an injected neighbour carrying
# NTF_ROUTER, the flag the kernel sets when it receives a Router Advertisement
# (RFC 4861). handleNeighUpdate (internal/plugins/iface/netlink/
# monitor_linux.go) turns it into router-discovered and handleRouterDiscovered
# (internal/component/iface/register.go) installs ::/0 at the unit's
# route-priority. Injecting the neighbour rather than sending a real RA keeps
# the subject of this test the carrier queue.
#
# There is deliberately no reject= on the resync's own log line. That line is
# log.Info and the functional runner starts the daemon above Info, so such a
# reject could never fire; the counter is the readable form of the same fact.
#
# needs-linux:caps=net-admin: creating a veth pair, moving a carrier and
# installing routes all need CAP_NET_ADMIN, which the verify runner does not
# have. Runs as root under `make ze-qemu-needs-linux-test`.
#
# KERNEL STATE THIS TEST OWNS: the veth pair zeflapv0/zeflapv1 and the dummy
# zeflapd0, all created from the config and deleted by the daemon at shutdown;
# the address block 2001:db8:f1a9::/64; the neighbour fe80::f1a9 on zeflapv0;
# and the IPv6 default route through it. Nothing else under test/ names those
# devices or that prefix. The default route is node-wide for as long as the run
# lasts, which is the one piece of state here that is not device-local: it sits
# at metric 254 through a veth whose far end is this test's own, and it goes
# with the device at shutdown.
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin

tmpfs=iface-link-flap-during-commit.run:mode=755:terminator=EOF_RUN
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Flap a carrier through a running daemon while a commit blocks its link worker.

The burst goes through `ip -batch`, one process for all 101 transitions, so the
kernel produces them faster than the worker could reprogram a route for each
one. A shell loop forking `ip` 101 times would not.
"""

import os
import re
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import urllib.request

from ze_api import API
from ze_api import runtime_fail

DEV = "zeflapv0"
PEER = "zeflapv1"
GATEWAY = "fe80::f1a9"
LLADDR = "02:00:00:00:f1:a9"
# The route-priority the config writes on DEV's unit.
BASE_METRIC = 254
# deprioritizedMetric (internal/component/iface/register.go). Hardcoded on
# purpose: if that constant moves, this test fails loudly and says which number
# it expected, which a silently-derived offset would hide.
DEPRIORITIZED_OFFSET = 1024
DOWN_METRIC = BASE_METRIC + DEPRIORITIZED_OFFSET
# Odd, and the first line is down, so the link settles DOWN. See the header for
# both bounds on this number.
TRANSITIONS = 101
ROUNDS = 6
# How much of the commit to let run before the burst starts. The reload has to
# reach the dhcpMu block, and the hold that follows lasts 1.1-3.3s, so the
# whole burst lands inside it.
COMMIT_SETTLE = 1.0
# Two rate-tracker ticks. The tracker pushes live carrier once a second, so a
# repair this round needed has been counted by the time this expires.
RESYNC_WINDOW = 1.6
METRICS_URL = "http://127.0.0.1:$PORT2/metrics"


def run(args, check=True):
    result = subprocess.run(args, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if check and result.returncode != 0:
        runtime_fail(f'{" ".join(args)}: exit {result.returncode}: {result.stderr.strip()}')
    return result


def wait_until(what, probe, timeout=20.0, interval=0.02):
    """Poll probe until it returns something other than None."""
    deadline = time.monotonic() + timeout
    while time.monotonic() < deadline:
        value = probe()
        if value is not None:
            return value
        time.sleep(interval)
    runtime_fail(f'timed out after {timeout}s waiting for {what}')


def carrier(dev):
    """The kernel's live carrier for dev: "1", "0", or None when unreadable."""
    try:
        with open(f'/sys/class/net/{dev}/carrier') as handle:
            return handle.read().strip()
    except OSError:
        return None


def default_metric():
    """The metric the kernel holds ze's IPv6 default route on DEV at."""
    out = run(["ip", "-6", "route", "show", "default", "dev", DEV]).stdout
    found = re.search(r'\bmetric (\d+)', out)
    return int(found.group(1)) if found else None


def netlink_drops():
    """Total Drops across every netlink socket. The kernel dropping a link
    notification is a loss ahead of ze, not in its queue, and it would make the
    resync assertion unreachable for any producer."""
    with open("/proc/net/netlink") as handle:
        lines = handle.read().splitlines()
    if not lines:
        return -1
    header = lines[0].split()
    if "Drops" not in header:
        return -1
    col = header.index("Drops")
    total = 0
    for line in lines[1:]:
        cols = line.split()
        if len(cols) > col:
            try:
                total += int(cols[col])
            except ValueError:
                pass
    return total


def scrape():
    with urllib.request.urlopen(METRICS_URL, timeout=5.0) as resp:
        return resp.read().decode()


def counter(body, metric, name):
    """The value of metric{name="<name>"}, or None when it has no such sample."""
    pattern = re.compile(
        r'^' + re.escape(metric) + r'\{[^}]*name="' + re.escape(name) + r'"[^}]*\} ([0-9.e+-]+)$',
        re.M)
    found = pattern.search(body)
    return float(found.group(1)) if found else None


def coalesced_now():
    return counter(scrape(), 'ze_iface_link_events_coalesced_total', DEV) or 0.0


def resyncs_now():
    return counter(scrape(), 'ze_iface_carrier_resyncs_total', DEV) or 0.0


def commit(pid):
    """Flip the DHCP hostname and reload. The flip is what makes this a real
    config change: reconcileDHCP then stops and starts every client on zeflapd0
    under dhcpMu, which is the lock the link worker takes for every apply. A
    SIGHUP over an unchanged config never reaches that code."""
    path = "ze-bgp.conf"
    with open(path) as handle:
        text = handle.read()
    text = text.replace("hostname flap-a", "hostname flap-Z") \
               .replace("hostname flap-b", "hostname flap-a") \
               .replace("hostname flap-Z", "hostname flap-b")
    with open(path, "w") as handle:
        handle.write(text)
    os.kill(pid, signal.SIGHUP)


def dispatch(api, command):
    try:
        resp = api._call_engine(
            'ze-plugin-engine:dispatch-command', {'command': command})
    except Exception as e:  # unwired command -> loud RED, never a vacuous pass
        return {'status': 'error', 'error': f'engine call raised: {e}'}
    if resp is None:
        return {'status': 'error', 'error': 'engine call returned None'}
    return resp.get('result', {})


def main():
    api = API()
    api.declare_done()
    api.wait_for_config()
    api.capability_done()
    api.wait_for_registry()
    api.ready()
    api.wait_for_post_startup(timeout=10.0)

    try:
        # ze creates the pair from the config. Both ends go up here: the daemon
        # owns DEV, and PEER is only the far side of the segment, which nothing
        # configures. Moving PEER's ADMIN state is what moves DEV's CARRIER
        # while DEV stays admin up, so its address and connected route survive.
        wait_until(
            f'{DEV} and {PEER} to exist',
            lambda: True if os.path.exists(f'/sys/class/net/{DEV}')
            and os.path.exists(f'/sys/class/net/{PEER}') else None)
        run(["ip", "link", "set", DEV, "up"])
        run(["ip", "link", "set", PEER, "up"])
        wait_until(f'{DEV} carrier to come up',
                   lambda: True if carrier(DEV) == "1" else None)

        # `nud permanent` keeps the kernel from probing this neighbour into
        # NUD_FAILED, which would emit router-lost and take the route away.
        run(["ip", "-6", "neigh", "replace", GATEWAY, "lladdr", LLADDR,
             "dev", DEV, "router", "nud", "permanent"])
        wait_until(
            f'the daemon to install ::/0 via {GATEWAY} on {DEV} at metric {BASE_METRIC}',
            lambda: True if default_metric() == BASE_METRIC else None)

        batch = os.path.abspath("flap.batch")
        with open(batch, "w") as handle:
            for i in range(TRANSITIONS):
                handle.write(f'link set {PEER} {"down" if i % 2 == 0 else "up"}\n')
        with open("daemon.pid") as handle:
            pid = int(handle.read().strip())

        drops_before = netlink_drops()
        resyncs_before = resyncs_now()
        stalled = 0
        pressures = []

        for round_no in range(ROUNDS):
            pressure_before = coalesced_now()

            commit(pid)
            time.sleep(COMMIT_SETTLE)
            run(["ip", "-force", "-batch", batch])

            # The worker is blocked on dhcpMu, so the route has not moved yet.
            # Counted rather than asserted per round: a faster machine could
            # release the lock first, and the pressure assertion below is the
            # guard that keeps this test honest either way.
            if default_metric() == BASE_METRIC:
                stalled += 1

            wait_until(
                f'round {round_no}: the default route on {DEV} to reach metric {DOWN_METRIC}',
                lambda: True if default_metric() == DOWN_METRIC else None,
                timeout=15.0)
            time.sleep(RESYNC_WINDOW)

            pressure = coalesced_now() - pressure_before
            pressures.append(pressure)
            if pressure <= 0:
                runtime_fail(
                    f'round {round_no}: ze_iface_link_events_coalesced_total for {DEV} did not '
                    f'move, so the burst never outran the worker and this run says nothing '
                    f'about a queue under pressure')
            if carrier(DEV) != "0" or default_metric() != DOWN_METRIC:
                runtime_fail(
                    f'round {round_no}: carrier={carrier(DEV)!r} metric={default_metric()!r}, '
                    f'want carrier "0" at metric {DOWN_METRIC}')

            run(["ip", "link", "set", PEER, "up"])
            wait_until(f'round {round_no}: carrier back up',
                       lambda: True if carrier(DEV) == "1" else None)
            wait_until(f'round {round_no}: the route back at metric {BASE_METRIC}',
                       lambda: True if default_metric() == BASE_METRIC else None)
            time.sleep(RESYNC_WINDOW)

        dropped = netlink_drops() - drops_before
        if dropped != 0:
            runtime_fail(
                f'the kernel dropped {dropped} netlink notifications during this run: those '
                f'transitions never reached ze, so the resync assertion below would judge the '
                f'socket rather than the queue. Lower TRANSITIONS.')

        resyncs = resyncs_now() - resyncs_before
        if resyncs != 0:
            runtime_fail(
                f'ze_iface_carrier_resyncs_total for {DEV} rose by {resyncs:g}, want 0: the '
                f'route metric contradicted live carrier and the resync had to repair it, '
                f'which means the event path LOST a transition. The metric is right now only '
                f'because the self-heal put it there.')
        if stalled < ROUNDS // 2:
            runtime_fail(
                f'only {stalled} of {ROUNDS} rounds found the worker still blocked when the '
                f'burst ended: the commit is no longer stalling it, so these rounds no longer '
                f'reproduce the condition AC-5 names')

        print(f'OK: {ROUNDS} rounds of {TRANSITIONS} transitions during commits, '
              f'stalled={stalled}, pressure={pressures}, resyncs=0', file=sys.stderr)
    finally:
        run(["ip", "-6", "neigh", "del", GATEWAY, "dev", DEV], check=False)
        run(["ip", "link", "set", PEER, "up"], check=False)
        dispatch(api, 'request shutdown')
        api.wait_for_shutdown()


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
EOF_RUN

stdin=ze-conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	external iface-link-flap-during-commit {
		run ./iface-link-flap-during-commit.run
		encoder json
	}
}

# route-priority is what makes ze own this interface's IPv6 default routes:
# suppressRAForConfig publishes it and handleRouterDiscovered installs ::/0 at
# it. Without it the kernel keeps its own RA default routes and the daemon has
# no route to move (writtenRoutePriorities, internal/component/iface/register.go).
#
# zeflapd0 carries forty DHCP clients that will never find a server. They exist
# for their RESTART cost, not for a lease: each commit flips their hostname, so
# reconcileDHCP stops and starts all forty inside dhcpMu and the link worker
# waits 1.1-3.3s for that lock. That wait is the condition AC-5 names.
interface {
	backend netlink
	veth zeflapv0 {
		peer zeflapv1
		unit 0 {
			route-priority 254
			ipv6 {
				address [ 2001:db8:f1a9::1/64 ]
			}
		}
	}
	dummy zeflapd0 {
		unit 0 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 1 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 2 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 3 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 4 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 5 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 6 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 7 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 8 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 9 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 10 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 11 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 12 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 13 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 14 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 15 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 16 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 17 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 18 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 19 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 20 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 21 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 22 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 23 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 24 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 25 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 26 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 27 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 28 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 29 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 30 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 31 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 32 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 33 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 34 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 35 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 36 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 37 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 38 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
		unit 39 { ipv4 { dhcp { enabled true; hostname flap-a } } }
	}
}

# $PORT2, not a literal: a hardcoded telemetry port sits outside the per-test
# range the runner leases, so another concurrent test can take it and every
# scrape here fails on a collision that has nothing to do with carrier events.
telemetry {
	prometheus {
		enabled true
		server flap {
			ip 127.0.0.1
			port $PORT2
		}
	}
}

bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65000
			}
			router-id 10.0.0.1
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-conf:timeout=180s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=OK: 6 rounds of 101 transitions during commits
reject=stderr:pattern=panic
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
reject=stderr:pattern=unknown method
